Are you willing to fly
this into a firework

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.320
as it's exploding?

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.153
- That's the goal.

00:01:14.153 --> 00:01:16.380
If I can catch it, that's the hope.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.713
But we set it up just for that.
So I think we could get it.

00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:22.380
(drone buzzing)

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.550
- The first recorded instance
of something like a firework

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.340
occurred in China over 2000 years ago

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.040
where people would get a piece of bamboo

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.850
with the ends closed and they
would throw it in a fire.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.870
So as it heated up,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.700
everything inside would get hot and expand

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.580
and eventually burst, creating this bang,

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.640
which they thought
scared off evil spirits.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.230
Then they developed black powder

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.090
and they put black
powder inside the bamboo

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.890
to make an even bigger bang.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.006
That was much more impressive.

00:01:52.006 --> 00:01:56.370
I wanna test out the earliest
known recipe for gunpowder.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.560
It involves three ingredients

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.780
that you can find out in nature.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.060
The first is potassium nitrate,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.610
which actually comes
from bat and bird guano.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.970
Once they poop in the cave
and it dissolves in water,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.160
you get these white crystals coming out.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.080
The second ingredient is sulfur.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.800
These yellow crystals you can
just find on the side of hills

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.300
or around thermal vents.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.430
And the third ingredient is honey.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.130
That is the major fuel for the gunpowder.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.900
Now I'm gonna mix this all up.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.580
Looks like it could be frosting.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:27.993
So yummy.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.350
Earliest known recipe for gunpowder.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.890
What people observed was when
they heated up this mixture,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.520
it would spontaneously catch fire.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.520
I have no idea how big of
a thing this is gonna be.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.290
This is only about a gram of it,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.150
so I don't think it's gonna be huge.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.430
As we heat up that very
primitive gunpowder,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.560
we should see it burst into flames.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.490
It's gotta be close.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.720
Here we go. Here we go.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.830
Yes, we have made gunpowder.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.590
Now that took a long time
before we saw the fire.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.570
So why is that?

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.030
Well, honey contains a lot of water

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.100
and that's gonna slow down the rate

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.260
at which this is gonna start to combust.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.740
People figured out that a
better fuel was charcoal.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.290
There are three things
you need to make fire,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.500
fuel, oxygen and heat.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.040
In most fires, the oxygen
comes from the air,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.180
but in gunpowder,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.540
the oxygen is supplied by
one of the solid ingredients.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.790
The potassium nitrate
gives you the oxygen.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.640
It's KNO3, so there's a
lot of oxygen in here.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.670
And then the charcoal provides
the fuel, that carbon.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.920
Charcoal is basically just carbon,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.460
but it works better than other
forms of carbon like graphite

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.686
because it contains
these microscopic pores

00:03:41.686 --> 00:03:44.940
and that enables the
reactants to mix much better.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.490
They can actually get inside those pores.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.030
So if you mix potassium
nitrate with the fuel, charcoal

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.860
and add heat from a lighter,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.070
you should have all the
ingredients you need to make fire.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.653
So the question is, will this burn?

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.670
It seems like little bits
of it are catching on fire

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.860
and just like sparking up from there.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.450
But the trouble with this reaction

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.030
is that it has a high activation energy.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.060
So it needs a lot of heat to get going.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.470
Whoa.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:14.433
There it goes.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.726
That is cool.

00:04:17.726 --> 00:04:20.476
(cheerful music)

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.150
I can see little balls of molten stuff

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.140
in the crucible there.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.463
That is awesome.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.540
So we do have oxygen and fuel
and we can get that reaction,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.440
but it's just not a
very explosive reaction.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.510
This is where the sulfur comes in.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.090
Sulfur starts reacting
with the other ingredients

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:43.020
at lower temperatures, and
those reactions give off heat.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.850
The sulfur is gonna act like kindling.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.683
It's gonna create that initial bit of heat

00:04:46.683 --> 00:04:49.920
that the potassium nitrate
needs to really break it open

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.690
and then, you know, everything can react.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.060
So that's what I wanna do,
I wanna try to mix sulfur,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.800
potassium nitrate and charcoal,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.850
which is like your quintessential
recipe for gunpowder.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.560
And the amounts that you need of this

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.100
have been pretty stable
for like 800 years,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.100
which is about 75% potassium
nitrate, 10% sulfur,

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.870
and 15% charcoal.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.550
Those are the right mixtures

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:17.100
so that you have just the right
number of each type of atom

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.760
to fully react and release
the most energy possible.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.240
So I wanna test how long it
takes for fire to burn down

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.660
about a meter of this homemade gunpowder.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.560
Let's let her rip.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.273
There it goes.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.803
Hey, it's going so slow.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.480
(suspenseful music)

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.940
Come on, you can do it.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.370
Go, go,

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.380
go, go, go.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.020
Wow, it made it to the end.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.640
You know what I think the problem is

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.640
the granules of charcoal that I've got

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:57.750
and these grains of potassium
nitrate, even the sulfur,

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.580
they're all a bit big.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.730
So to actually get that
reaction to happen fast,

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.460
it's not really working.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.410
So what I wanna do is compare that

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.500
to store bought gun powder from, you know,

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.900
a factory that's actually
ground up all those particles

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:13.920
to be really tiny.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.233
So all the reagents are
really close together.

00:06:18.483 --> 00:06:19.316
Oh my...

00:06:19.316 --> 00:06:20.670
- Whoo!

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.458
- That is impressive.

00:06:22.458 --> 00:06:25.458
(suspenseful music)

00:06:29.466 --> 00:06:30.360
(laughs)

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.355
Yes.

00:06:31.355 --> 00:06:33.690
That is gunpowder.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:36.633
That makes mine look so amateur.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.120
The chemical reaction that occurs

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.150
in the combustion of
gunpowder is complicated,

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.350
to say the least.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.680
But a number of the
products formed are solids,

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.880
and that's why burning gunpowder
creates a lot of smoke.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.520
The neighbors have gotta be
looking at this and being like-

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.167
- That is so...
- What the...

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:57.780
The thing about gun
powder is if you burn it

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.210
when it's just exposed to the air,

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:01.710
it doesn't really explode.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:04.050
And I think of gunpowder as explosive.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.020
But in order to see that, what
you have to do is confine it.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.060
Because when you confine gun powder,

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.940
you increase the concentration
of all the reagents.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.920
They don't just blow away from each other

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:14.850
when they catch fire.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.790
So they're forced into closer
proximity, so they react more.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:19.870
Plus you trap more of the heat,

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:23.520
so the reagents get moving
faster, they have more energy,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.321
and so they can react more readily.

00:07:26.321 --> 00:07:30.330
So I put 10 grams of
gunpowder in a cardboard tube

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:31.653
and taped it shut.

00:07:33.296 --> 00:07:34.950
(explosion pops)

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.150
Okay, that's better.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.958
But it wasn't as big of a
bang as I was hoping for.

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.460
Well, it was a bit more
of a pop than than a bang.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.410
Maybe we need a tighter vessel, I think.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.170
So I'm gonna use some
fiber reinforced tape

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.000
to strengthen the container,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.850
and then we should get a bigger bang.

00:07:51.578 --> 00:07:53.670
(explosion booms)
Ooh!

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:54.964
- It went right past me.

00:07:54.964 --> 00:07:56.940
(explosion booms)

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.160
- It's more of a bang.
That's a bit more like it.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.110
Here I have 20 grams of black powder.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:04.320
So I guess the question is
with twice the black powder,

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.030
do we get twice the bang?

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.013
Here we go, everyone get back.

00:08:10.188 --> 00:08:13.271
(explosion booming)

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:19.080
This is essentially what a firework is.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.390
I mean, fireworks consist of a shell

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.760
with a lot of gunpowder inside.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.590
- So, this is an inert shell.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.490
It's colorized

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.860
so that you can see the
individual pieces better.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.170
A real shell, everything
in here would be black

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:34.200
because it would be primed
or covered with black powder.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.167
- Actually a note about terminology.

00:08:36.167 --> 00:08:39.360
For a long time the terms
gunpowder and black powder

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.220
were used in interchangeably.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.100
But these days the formula
for gunpowder has changed

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.630
to make it smokeless.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:47.880
So what's used in fireworks is called,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.890
at least in the US, black powder,

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.820
to distinguish it from
modern smokeless gunpowder.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.010
Just know that in this
video we'll use both terms

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.110
to mean basically the same thing.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.180
- The shell itself is made out a cardboard

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.750
and what looks like and
is a form of paper mache

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.330
with craft paper and a glue.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:07.740
And you go around and around

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.510
once you put the two
shell halves together.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.700
- Is that like the standard
size you'd see when you go out?

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.040
- So on a 4th of July display,

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.710
you'll see anything
from two and a half inch

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.480
up to five inch typically.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.540
In most cases, six inch and above

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.410
are reserved for locations
that you have a huge property,

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.270
you're out in some desert shoot site

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:28.590
or on a barge somewhere.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:31.433
But they can go even bigger
than this, up to 12 inch, 16

00:09:31.433 --> 00:09:34.410
and even larger in very special occasions.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.535
This is the hemisphere for a
16 inch diameter aerial shell.

00:09:38.535 --> 00:09:40.055
- That is a huge firework.

00:09:40.055 --> 00:09:44.910
The biggest aerial firework
ever was 1.44 meters wide,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.010
and it weighed about the same as a car.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:49.981
(speaker gasping)

00:09:49.981 --> 00:09:52.301
- Ah-ooh-ooh-ooh!
- Whoooh!

00:09:52.301 --> 00:09:53.134
- Whoa.

00:09:53.134 --> 00:09:54.330
(all laughing)

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.520
- To launch a firework into the air,

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.000
the way it's done is
with more black powder.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:00.960
- In a typical shell,

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.820
you're gonna have a baggie of black powder

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.010
that's underneath the shell,

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.500
and that acts to fire the
shell out of the mortar

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.120
to the 800 feet in the air,

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.950
which is what this eight
inch shell would go to.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:13.080
Shells as large as say a 16 inch,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.600
those can use literally
pounds of black powder

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:16.743
underneath them.

00:10:16.743 --> 00:10:19.680
- To ensure the shell goes straight up.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.520
It's placed in a big plastic
tube called a mortar.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.560
- A pyro technician gets two bangs

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.540
for every one the audience does.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.370
You get a bang when the mortar fires

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.382
and then a bang when the shell explodes.

00:10:31.382 --> 00:10:34.465
(explosions booming)

00:10:36.853 --> 00:10:38.070
And it's literally like a cannon,

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.660
shooting a cannon ball in the air.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:42.450
So the audience doesn't get
to hear or see or feel that,

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:43.811
but the pyro crew does.

00:10:43.811 --> 00:10:46.894
(explosions booming)

00:10:54.406 --> 00:10:57.406
(audience cheering)

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:00.840
- Does everything burn up
before it reaches the ground?

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.460
- The internal contents, yes.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.200
So all the stars, the
burst, all of that stuff,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.510
the casing will come down in pieces.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.390
That's why we have a fallout
zone is what we call it.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:10.770
Or the exclusion zone,

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.410
where only the crew is
allowed with all their PPEs

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.720
because you do get debris that comes down.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.330
It literally sounds like rain at the end

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.940
from all of the debris from that finale,

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.863
shells going off all at once.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:26.280
- How often do they malfunction?

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.840
- It's rare, but it does happen.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:31.110
You'll have a wooden rack with a few HDPE,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.097
high density polyethylene mortars in it.

00:11:33.097 --> 00:11:36.990
And that particular
material is meant to expand

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:40.080
and split from the energy
of a malfunctioning shell

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:42.925
versus explode and throw
shrapnel everywhere.

00:11:42.925 --> 00:11:45.960
- We actually experienced
an incident like this

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.510
with the biggest shell
we had set to launch.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:50.070
Okay, in three,

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:50.970
two,

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:52.166
one.

00:11:52.166 --> 00:11:52.999
(explosion pops)

00:11:52.999 --> 00:11:54.630
Oh, oh.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:56.900
That one did not look like it...

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.147
(explosion booms)

00:11:58.147 --> 00:12:00.480
Oh!
(laughs)

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.020
That's what happens when
a firework doesn't lift.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.540
- But because we follow all
the rules and regulations

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:07.830
at a professional display,

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:10.410
it's usually the crowd going, whoa.

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:12.198
And the crew going, whoa.

00:12:12.198 --> 00:12:13.176
(explosion booms)

00:12:13.176 --> 00:12:14.496
- Oh my God.

00:12:14.496 --> 00:12:17.250
(both laughing)

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.381
- And then the show goes on

00:12:18.381 --> 00:12:21.120
is typically what happens
after a few safety checks

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:22.440
when something like that goes on.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.030
- Thankfully everyone was safe

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:25.950
because we could trigger
the fireworks remotely

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:28.170
from outside the blast radius.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:32.313
But igniting explosives safely
has long been a challenge.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:34.980
- In the mining industry,

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:36.690
their delay method back then

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.960
was to literally sprinkle
out a trail of black powder

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.750
through the mine to give
them a delay to the dynamite.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.750
- The simplest fuse just
consists of cotton string

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.490
that's been soaked in black powder

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:49.410
and then allowed to dry out.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:51.703
This is known as black match.

00:12:51.703 --> 00:12:54.780
It burns well, but pretty slowly.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.900
- That alone would burn two
to three seconds an inch.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.330
But once you encase it in craft paper,

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.010
that's literally all this is,

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.200
you change that burn rate
from a couple seconds an inch

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.560
to anywhere from 30 to
a hundred feet a second.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:08.850
- This has the same effect

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.470
as containing the black powder.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:12.360
It traps in the heat and reagents,

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.190
so the reaction goes much faster.

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:17.190
- This is actually how we
shoot our finale so fast.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.770
We're not actually pushing
buttons that quickly.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:23.640
- This type of fuse is
appropriately known as quick match.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.550
Now, before I'd ever seen
quick match in action,

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.770
it was suggested that I
try to race the signal

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:30.420
down the fuse.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.253
What I'm gonna do

00:13:31.253 --> 00:13:33.030
is light this little piece
of black match right here,

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:36.690
and then I'm gonna try to race
the signal to the other end.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.868
But still, I was not
prepared for what came next.

00:13:39.868 --> 00:13:41.089
(suspenseful music)

00:13:41.089 --> 00:13:43.650
(explosions popping)
Ah! Jesus,

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:45.321
what the heck was that?

00:13:45.321 --> 00:13:50.321
(suspenseful music)
(explosions popping)

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:54.603
You are kidding me.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.990
You didn't tell me it was gonna do that.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:13:59.850
The force of the combustion products

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:03.360
causes the quick match
to whip around wildly.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:06.750
It's crazy how much just
containing the reagents

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:11.340
and containing the heat from
the reaction accelerates it.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:13.680
It certainly burns much
faster than the black match

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.174
without the paper.

00:14:15.174 --> 00:14:16.293
(explosion pops)
Ah!

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.683
Man. Every time, every time that gets me.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:24.120
One problem with these basic fuses

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.833
is they aren't waterproof.

00:14:26.689 --> 00:14:29.490
Even though they contain
the fuel and the oxidizer,

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:32.340
so they don't need the
oxygen and the air to burn,

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:36.030
they do need heat and
water is just too good

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.370
at conducting the heat away from the fuse.

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:40.920
So it snuffs out the reaction.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.270
And it fizzles out.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.943
The same problem occurs with road flares.

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:50.220
I thought since the flame comes
out with such high pressure,

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.770
it might be able to stay lit under water.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:53.670
Three,

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:54.553
two,

00:14:54.553 --> 00:14:55.436
one.

00:14:55.436 --> 00:14:58.860
(water splashes)

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:00.993
And it did for a little while,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.760
but eventually the water got
in there and stole the heat.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.700
So I think that the
water is putting it out

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:10.080
by pulling the heat away from it

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.723
so it doesn't have enough
energy to keep burning.

00:15:12.723 --> 00:15:16.020
(water burbling)

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:16.870
You bring it out.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:19.080
Come on, come on.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:19.913
Ah.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.090
- Wait, look.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:23.142
- He-ey!

00:15:23.142 --> 00:15:23.975
(both laugh)

00:15:23.975 --> 00:15:25.308
- Ooh!

00:15:29.277 --> 00:15:30.110
- And it's...

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:32.520
(water burbling)

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:36.630
There are special flares made
for underwater applications,

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:39.273
but even those have similar problems.

00:15:41.408 --> 00:15:44.158
(water burbling)

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:47.970
You can see the gaseous
combustion products

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.730
being released here and
bubbling to the surface.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:52.890
And while this flare lasted longer,

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.443
the water still eventually put it out.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:58.353
Come on. Why did that go out?

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.373
But it is possible to
make a waterproof fuse.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.272
- So this is your traditional visco fuse.

00:16:05.272 --> 00:16:08.632
You're gonna see this in a lot
of your consumer fireworks.

00:16:08.632 --> 00:16:11.049
(calm music)

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:13.982
- Very big firework store.

00:16:13.982 --> 00:16:16.732
(cheerful music)

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.220
I feel like we have to get this, spins?

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:25.053
- Yeah.

00:16:25.053 --> 00:16:26.860
- And it makes fire?

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:27.693
- Yeah.

00:16:27.693 --> 00:16:29.700
- I'm excited about this one.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.254
Here we go in three,

00:16:31.254 --> 00:16:32.280
two,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:33.113
one.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.200
- [Mike] It's really got a powder core

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.675
with a fiber wrap to it.

00:16:39.675 --> 00:16:42.675
(fireworks popping)

00:16:46.984 --> 00:16:49.317
(laughing)

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.580
And it's typically lacquer coated.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.073
So these fuses are typically waterproof.

00:16:57.164 --> 00:17:00.164
(explosion popping)

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:02.693
- Whoa!

00:17:02.693 --> 00:17:05.333
(water burbling)

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.116
Wow!

00:17:10.116 --> 00:17:10.949
(laughs)

00:17:10.949 --> 00:17:12.540
That was impressive.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.360
Fuses are not only used
to light fireworks,

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:18.840
they are a core component
inside a firework.

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.610
- So that quick match flashes really quick

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:23.490
down into the black
powder lift that ignites.

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.040
And while it's pushing the
shell out of the mortar,

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:27.570
it lights the time fuse.

00:17:27.570 --> 00:17:30.330
So this is what would
actually be in the shell

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:31.830
to give you the time delay.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:33.399
From the time the lift charge fires

00:17:33.399 --> 00:17:36.960
to the time the shell hits
apogee and explodes in the sky.

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:38.730
You know, it's about a
quarter inch in diameter

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:43.230
and is really a black
powder core rigid fuse

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.660
compared to the more flimsy visco type.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:47.970
- It's really important
that the time delay fuse

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.160
keeps the combustion contained inside it

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.920
so the firework only
explodes when you want it to,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.590
at the very peak of its trajectory.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.780
So we tested it underwater.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.160
Do you think it's waterproof?

00:17:59.160 --> 00:17:59.993
- Should be.

00:17:59.993 --> 00:18:01.380
- All right. It's taking its time.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:02.213
There we go.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.880
That looks like a firework type fuse.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:06.713
- [Mike] These function

00:18:06.713 --> 00:18:08.940
by not spitting any fire out the side

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.089
so that the shell doesn't
get lit prematurely.

00:18:11.089 --> 00:18:11.922
- That's smart.

00:18:11.922 --> 00:18:14.310
- [Mike] So you should only
see it spit out the end

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:15.750
once it's made it's all the way through.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:16.890
- What's containing it?

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:18.982
Like what kind of material
is containing it in there?

00:18:18.982 --> 00:18:20.040
- [Mike] Well, there's a number...

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.550
There's weaves of fibers in
there and a layer of asphalt

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:25.443
that really provides that waterproofing.

00:18:25.443 --> 00:18:26.910
- So you can kind of see

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:28.290
how the signal is progressing.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.450
You can see how the fire's
progressing through there.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:32.190
'Cause obviously it's getting hot.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:34.340
Some of the gaseous
products are coming up.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:36.770
Oh, is it gonna come out?

00:18:39.457 --> 00:18:40.680
Was that it?

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:41.730
- [Mike] That was it.

00:18:42.963 --> 00:18:44.610
- That is all that is needed

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:46.921
to ignite the core of a firework.

00:18:46.921 --> 00:18:48.360
(both laugh)

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.070
- So after that time fuse hits its end,

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:51.750
it'll light a piece of black match

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:54.270
that'll then flash into the burst charge.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:56.490
So that's what's represented
by this blue material,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:59.603
which is typically rice hulls
or something similar to that

00:18:59.603 --> 00:19:01.830
coated with black powder.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:02.663
- What are ri...

00:19:02.663 --> 00:19:03.496
Oh rice hulls.

00:19:03.496 --> 00:19:04.973
- Yes. Rice hulls, not holes, sorry.

00:19:04.973 --> 00:19:07.890
- I was like they're making
rice holes like donut holes.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:09.540
- The reason we coat rice hulls

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:12.120
is because you get a very granular powder

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:13.980
in the center of the shell now,

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:16.890
which allows the flame
to propagate very quickly

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:20.100
through the shell, igniting
everything simultaneously,

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:23.880
creating that significant
over pressure very quickly

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:25.440
to blow the shell open.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.090
So a traditional peony shell

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.250
would be your most basic of fireworks,

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:32.342
where these stars would literally
be lining the hemisphere

00:19:32.342 --> 00:19:34.890
and then you'd have
your burst in the middle

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:36.030
and that would give you

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:38.520
a very symmetrical
round sphere in the sky,

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:40.770
your traditional firework that you see.

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:42.150
- But it's just interesting to think

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:44.910
that when you see a firework go off,

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:46.770
you know, all those little points of light

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:48.570
are about that size.

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:49.403
- [Mike] Yeah.

00:19:49.403 --> 00:19:50.400
- But they look...

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:52.260
I mean I would say that
they look bigger, right?

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:53.880
'Cause they're so bright, I guess.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:54.713
- Yeah.

00:19:54.713 --> 00:19:57.030
(drone whistling)

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:57.990
- So fast.

00:19:57.990 --> 00:19:59.867
- How fast does this thing go?

00:19:59.867 --> 00:20:02.550
- Pretty quick and it
is definitely trickier

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.113
when it's dark like this.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.487
- Okay, can you see anything?

00:20:06.487 --> 00:20:07.920
- [Gene] Once I go start looking here,

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:09.240
I'm like, I'm not sure.

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:12.240
- How do you get focused to
something that's not there yet

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.640
and you're not quite sure where
it is or where anything is

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:15.707
'cause it's all pitch black?

00:20:15.707 --> 00:20:18.420
- 'Cause I basically have
locked exposure on this.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.910
And we can at least see
how the exposure is.

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:21.910
- Taking off.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:24.210
(drone whistling)
(fireworks popping)

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:25.043
- Yes.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:27.593
Amazing.

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:31.114
Blue.

00:20:31.114 --> 00:20:33.531
(calm music)

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:38.577
- Oh shoot, okay.

00:20:38.577 --> 00:20:39.828
- Oh, dang.
- FPV's down.

00:20:39.828 --> 00:20:40.661
- [Gene] Oh dang.

00:20:40.661 --> 00:20:41.970
I didn't realize I was
so close to the ground.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:42.803
All right, hold on.

00:20:42.803 --> 00:20:44.880
- I just wanna see what
this looks like, you know,

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.503
I wanna check this out. So.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.680
Whoa, that is cool.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.240
I dig that.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.430
These stars are made of black powder

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:59.130
mixed with chemicals to give
them different properties.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:00.990
- [Mike] So brocade crown star

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:03.330
would be the big bushy gold tail

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:04.800
that you would see in the sky.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:08.457
So imagine a big glittering gold shell.

00:21:08.457 --> 00:21:10.470
(indistinct chatters)

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.990
- I could actually see the
little pieces coming off

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:13.953
and glowing pretty bright right now.

00:21:13.953 --> 00:21:15.810
- [Mike] Now, imagine those leaving a tail

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:17.010
as they fly through the sky.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.203
It's a really spectacular effect.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:21.753
- This is gonna be blue.

00:21:23.195 --> 00:21:24.390
Ah.

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:26.550
Often the chemicals mixed into the stars

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:28.833
are used to create specific colors.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:30.780
- [Mike] Each of these stars

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.000
would be coated with a primer composition,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:35.430
which is a really fine black powder.

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:37.619
The reason for that is a
lot of color compositions

00:21:37.619 --> 00:21:40.410
take more heat to ignite,

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:42.720
so they have a higher
ignition temperature.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:46.020
So by coating with black
powder, with that primer,

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.540
you're ensuring ignition of all the stars,

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:51.838
which then transfer the fire
to that color composition.

00:21:51.838 --> 00:21:54.300
(calm music)

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:56.038
- And then it started taking off on you.

00:21:56.038 --> 00:21:57.840
(imitates firework)

00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:59.070
That's cool.

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:00.209
I like that.

00:22:00.209 --> 00:22:02.626
(calm music)

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:06.930
Jeez it's bright.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:08.250
The colors of fireworks

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:11.100
are actually thanks to quantum mechanics.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.740
An element absorbs energy
from the combustion

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:16.650
raising an electron to
a higher energy level.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.320
And there are only certain
transitions which are allowed.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:20.730
So when the electron drops back down

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.070
to a lower energy level,
it gives off light

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.770
with a specific energy of that transition,

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.290
which corresponds to a particular color.

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.690
Some elements are particularly well-suited

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:33.150
to create certain colors.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.070
So as the copper ions go in there,

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:38.850
they absorb energy from the
fire, from the combustion,

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.440
and then the electrons drop back down

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:41.670
to a lower energy level

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:44.130
and they give out that
blue light predominantly.

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:46.560
And that's why we get
such a great blue color.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:48.480
What a thing of beauty.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:49.920
This is just a little interjection

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.290
because I was looking at our flame tests

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:54.360
and the copper salt tests, you know,

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:57.450
anywhere you look, copper
fireworks are meant to be blue,

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:01.110
like bright blue, but those
flame tests look kind of green.

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:03.360
And so I didn't really
know what was going on,

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:05.820
but I was sitting here
playing with my kids

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.610
with the KiwiCo flame test kit.

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:10.290
I know like they did sponsor this video,

00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:12.420
but didn't tell me to put
a section like this in.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.760
But what I found is that, okay,

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:19.760
I'm using a copper salt
here and it starts out green

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:23.700
and then if you leave it for
a while, come zoom in here,

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:25.560
it starts to get blue.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:27.450
Oh, look at that blue.

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:30.630
To get the beautiful blue color.
I think you need more heat.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:32.700
I think it's a higher
temperature that we need

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.360
that we didn't achieve in our
flame tests out in the field.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:39.270
Here we got some calcium
chloride. Oh yeah.

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:42.780
Oh, that is a beautiful orange.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:43.613
Look at that.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:47.220
Here we're getting some potassium chloride

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:48.513
to give us some violet.

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:51.450
I'm not seeing too much violet,

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:53.580
but maybe just a little.

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:54.813
Barium chloride.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:00.690
Of course, that's just small scale.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:04.140
Our pyro technician mixed
up some color salt and fuel

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:05.070
in a mortar.

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.660
- [Mike] What's in there is
about two gallons of methanol

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:08.282
with boric acid on this one.

00:24:08.282 --> 00:24:09.900
(explosion booms)

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:11.280
(both laughing)

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:14.520
And copper(II) chloride on this one.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:16.410
- Three, two, one.

00:24:17.572 --> 00:24:18.405
Fire.
(explosion booms)

00:24:18.405 --> 00:24:19.238
Yeah!

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:21.885
That was awesome.

00:24:21.885 --> 00:24:23.430
(calm music)

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:26.700
That is the beauty of quantum mechanics.

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:28.080
The electron transitions

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.600
that give rise to the
color of fireworks are,

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:31.586
at least in my opinion,

00:24:31.586 --> 00:24:34.530
way cooler than colored smoke bombs.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:38.010
So the combustion products here are solid,

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:40.080
and that's why they make so much smoke.

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:40.913
In this case,

00:24:40.913 --> 00:24:43.983
there's just dye in the
smoke to give it its color.

00:24:45.756 --> 00:24:46.743
Yes.

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:49.680
To make so-called ghost shells,

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.860
the stars are coated in
layers of different chemicals,

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:55.743
which allows them to
change color mid-flight.

00:24:56.610 --> 00:25:00.150
- So assuming your shell
construction was very uniform,

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.240
your casing, all your
stars are the same mass,

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.610
you're gonna get a very
uniform explosion in the sky.

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:07.470
So it's really as simple as

00:25:07.470 --> 00:25:10.140
how you lay out the stars in the shell

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.210
and it'll break in that
pattern in the sky.

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:15.540
- So what's your favorite patterned?

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:17.520
- Jellyfish are pretty neat.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:18.600
If you've ever seen a jellyfish,

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.130
it really looks like a jellyfish.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:20.963
You get the dome

00:25:20.963 --> 00:25:23.250
and then you get tentacles
coming out the bottom.

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:24.090
- That sounds amazing.

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:24.923
- [Mike] Yeah.

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:28.800
- Do they just do it by changing the color

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:30.570
and position of the stars up here?

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:32.940
Like how do you get a
dome and then tentacles?

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:33.900
- If this was the shell

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.490
and you'd have another half this way,

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:37.654
only the top half would be covered-

00:25:37.654 --> 00:25:38.487
- Would be fully covered.

00:25:38.487 --> 00:25:39.320
- And that would be your dome.

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:41.190
And then you'd have a couple larger stars

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:43.249
to create the large tentacles
that come out the bottom.

00:25:43.249 --> 00:25:44.760
- Ah.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:46.860
These days fireworks
aren't usually triggered

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:49.980
by lighting a fuse by hand,
but by pushing a button.

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:51.030
- Each one of these buttons

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:53.370
would correspond to an
individual firing circuit

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:54.660
going out into the field.

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:56.520
- The button sends current down the wire

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:57.930
to an electric match.

00:25:57.930 --> 00:25:59.970
A little filament in the match heats up,

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:01.926
triggering a chemical reaction.

00:26:01.926 --> 00:26:03.630
- [Mike] Which then spits fire

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:05.480
into whatever you're trying to light.

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:10.590
- [Gene] Yeah, I think it's just...

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:11.730
- Dust in the motors?

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:12.870
- I think so.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:15.810
This might be a pretty quick repair.

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:19.680
- We are about to have a fireworks finale

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.674
in the middle of nowhere in the pitch dark

00:26:22.674 --> 00:26:24.660
for like a minute.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:29.430
And we're gonna try to
fly FPV drone through it.

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:32.264
So what does it actually
look like to be in the middle

00:26:32.264 --> 00:26:37.264
where all the fireworks
are exploding in three,

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:38.520
two,

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:39.693
one.

00:26:39.693 --> 00:26:44.693
(drone whistling)
(fireworks popping)

00:26:50.758 --> 00:26:52.024
- Thank you.

00:26:52.024 --> 00:26:55.024
(fireworks popping)

00:27:08.922 --> 00:27:13.922
(upbeat music)
(fireworks popping)

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:34.770
Wow.

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:36.856
Can we get some light on the ground here?

00:27:36.856 --> 00:27:38.610
(laughing)

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:40.127
- That was insane.

00:27:41.067 --> 00:27:43.770
- The fireworks finale
triggered my car alarm.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:45.457
- They were like two
times where I was like,

00:27:45.457 --> 00:27:47.347
"I'm not sure if I'm upside down or not,

00:27:47.347 --> 00:27:49.950
but I gotta hold this shot."

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:52.834
Okay, so

00:27:52.834 --> 00:27:54.094
- [All] Whoa.

00:27:54.094 --> 00:27:56.761
(hopeful music)

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:03.090
- Oh man. Right through
them, right around them.

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:04.932
Just parked right down into that.

00:28:04.932 --> 00:28:07.515
(all laughing)

00:28:08.945 --> 00:28:10.945
Whoa, that is so wicked.

00:28:12.316 --> 00:28:13.547
- Dude.

00:28:13.547 --> 00:28:15.008
- Yeah, there we go.

00:28:15.008 --> 00:28:16.936
(all laughing)

00:28:16.936 --> 00:28:17.970
